#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# variance

#% text
# `variance(xs)` gives the sample variance of the list of values `xs`, the sum of the squared deviations from the mean divided by n - 1.

#% text
# `variance(d)` gives the variance of the distribution `d`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Exact values give an exact variance, machine reals a machine real, and symbols a symbolic expression.

#% item
# For a list of rows, the variance is taken column by column.

#% item
# A list needs at least two elements.

#% item
# The variance of a distribution is exact, and symbolic for symbolic parameters.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The variance of four integers:

#% cell
variance([1, 2, 3, 4])
#% output
#= 5/3

#% text
# Of machine reals:

#% cell
variance([1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0])
#% output
#= 1.6666666666666667

#% text
# Of a distribution:

#% cell
variance(binomial_distribution(n, p))
#% output
#= n*p*(1 - p)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Column by column:

#% cell
variance([[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 9]])
#% output
#= [4, 13]

#% text
# Of symbols:

#% cell
variance([a, b])
#% output
#= (a + (-1/2)*(a + b))^2 + (b + (-1/2)*(a + b))^2

#% text
# Of several distributions:

#% cell
[variance(normal_distribution(mu, sigma)), variance(poisson_distribution(m)), variance(uniform_distribution([a, b])), variance(gamma_distribution(k, t))]
#% output
#= [sigma^2, m, (1/12)*(b - a)^2, k*t^2]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Compare the sample variance of simulated Poisson counts with the variance of the distribution, which equals its mean:

#% cell
seed(1); counts3 = random_variate(poisson_distribution(3), 5000); n([mean(counts3), variance(counts3)])
#% output
#= [3.0222, 2.935094178835767]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The sample variance is the second central moment times n/(n - 1):

#% cell
central_moment([1, 2, 3, 4], 2)*4/3
#% output
#= 5/3

#% text
# It is the square of the standard deviation:

#% cell
variance([2, 4, 4, 5]) == std_dev([2, 4, 4, 5])^2
#% output
#= true

#% text
# A shift of all the values does not change it, even with large values:

#% cell
[variance([1e9 + 1, 1e9 + 2, 1e9 + 3]), variance([10^20 + 1, 10^20 + 2, 10^20 + 3])]
#% output
#= [1.0, 1]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# One value has no sample variance:

#% cell
variance([7])
#% output
#! failure(argument, variance, "the list needs at least two elements")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `std_dev`, `mean`, `central_moment`, `moment`, `median`
